{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{
 "url": "https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/09/iphone-18-pro-iphone-duo-new-c2-modem/",
 "title": "iPhone 18 Pro and iPhone Duo Feature Apple's New C2 Modem [Updated] - MacRumors",
 "text": "iPhone 18 Pro and iPhone Duo Feature Apple's New C2 Modem [Updated]\nWednesday September 9, 2026 12:28 pm PDT by Tim Hardwick\nApple today unveiled new iPhone 18 Pro models and the iPhone Duo – the company's first foldable iPhone – and both the iPhone 18 Pro and the iPhone Duo feature Apple's new custom-designed C2 modem for 5G connectivity.\nPrior to today's announcements, there was speculation that Apple's C2 in-house modem would not support mmWave 5G in the U.S., and that the company would once again rely on Qualcomm to fill the gap for American carriers. Apparently, that's not the case, at least as far mmWave support goes.\nApple's press release for the iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max and the tech specs page for the two models appear to indicate that the C2 modem is limited to only the smaller iPhone 18 Pro model, leaving the iPhone 18 Pro Max on Qualcomm modems. The reason for the distinction is unclear given the near identical capabilities of the iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max.\nApple says the C2 brings AI-powered improvements to cellular quality and reliability, and now supports mmWave in the U.S. The company claims the C2 also delivers meaningfully faster uploads when compared to C1X while consuming 15 percent less energy.\nAs for mmWave support, Apple's U.S. specifications list bands n258, n260, and n261, alongside sub-6GHz 5G with 4×4 MIMO, but the UK specifications list sub-6GHz only.\nMeanwhile, both models feature the N1, an Apple-designed wireless networking chip that enables Wi-Fi 7, Bluetooth 6, and Thread support.\nThe iPhone 18 Pro and iPhone 18 Pro Max come in burgundy, glacier, silver, and black colors, with prices starting at $1,199 and $1,299, respectively. Both models come in 256GB, 512GB, 1TB, and 2TB storage options and will be available to order September 12.\niPhone Duo is available in star white and night sky colors, and starts at $1,999, coming in 256GB, 512GB, 1TB, and 2TB storage capacities. Pre-orders for the iPhone Duo begin on Friday, October 16, with availability beginning Friday, October 23.\nUpdate : This article originally stated that all three models include the C2 modem, but it appears the iPhone 18 Pro Max does not in fact include it.\nRelated Roundups: iPhone 18 Pro , iPhone Duo\nTags: Apple 5G Modem Guide , September 2026 Apple Event\nBuyer's Guide: iPhone 18 Pro (Buy Now) , iPhone Duo (Buy Now)\nRelated Forum: iPhone\n[ 60 comments ]",
 "truncated": false,
 "budget": "1 of 12 used for this brief"
}"}],"isError":false}

{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{
 "url": "https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/apple-intelligence-foundation-language-models",
 "title": "Apple Intelligence Foundation Language Models - Apple Machine Learning Research",
 "text": "research area Speech and Natural Language Processing\ncontent type paper published July 2024\nApple Intelligence Foundation Language Models\nView publication\nCopy Bibtex\nWe present foundation language models developed to power Apple Intelligence features, including a ∼3 billion parameter model designed to run efficiently on devices and a large server-based language model designed for Private Cloud Compute. These models are designed to perform a wide range of tasks efficiently, accurately, and responsibly. This report describes the model architecture, the data used to train the model, the training process, how the models are optimized for inference, and the evaluation results. We highlight our focus on Responsible AI and how the principles are applied throughout the model development.\nThis paper provides technical details for Apple’s On-Device and Server Foundation Models, introduced on June 10, 2024, in this post .\nRelated readings and updates.\nApple Intelligence Foundation Language Models Tech Report 2025\nJuly 17, 2025 research area Speech and Natural Language Processing\nWe introduce two multilingual, multimodal foundation language models that power Apple Intelligence features across Apple devices and services: (i) a ∼3B-parameter on-device model optimized for Apple silicon through architectural innovations such as KV-cache sharing and 2-bit quantization-aware training; and (ii) a scalable server model built on a novel Parallel-Track Mixture-of-Experts (PT-MoE) transformer that combines track parallelism,…\nRead more\nIntroducing Apple’s On-Device and Server Foundation Models\nJune 10, 2024\nAt the 2024 Worldwide Developers Conference , we introduced Apple Intelligence, a personal intelligence system integrated deeply into iOS 18, iPadOS 18, and macOS Sequoia.\nApple Intelligence is comprised of multiple highly-capable generative models that are specialized for our users’ everyday tasks, and can adapt on the fly for their current activity. The foundation models built into Apple Intelligence have been fine-tuned for user experiences such as writing and refining text, prioritizing and summarizing notifications, creating playful images for conversations with family and friends, and taking in-app actions to simplify interactions across apps.\nRead more\nDiscover opportunities in Machine Learning.\nOur research in machine learning breaks new ground every day.\nWork with us",
 "truncated": false,
