{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{
 "url": "https://piptheory.com/research/apple-q4-fy2026-earnings-preview-november-2-memory-costs-margin",
 "title": "47%–48% Margin Guided, Inventory Doubled to $11.1bn (Apple Earnings Preview, 2 November 2026): What to Expect From Q4 — and Why Memory Costs and the December Guide Decide the Read — Pip Theory",
 "text": "Photo by Arild Vågen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.\n47%–48% Margin Guided, Inventory Doubled to $11.1bn (Apple Earnings Preview, 2 November 2026): What to Expect From Q4 — and Why Memory Costs and the December Guide Decide the Read\nApple reports fiscal fourth-quarter results after the US close on Monday 2 November 2026, with the call at 5 p.m. Eastern (22:00 UTC, after the clocks change). It guided revenue growth of 9% to 11% — about $111.7bn to $113.7bn — and a gross margin of 47% to 48%, and published consensus sits near $113.3bn of revenue and $1.99 of EPS. The September quarter is largely spoken for; what the market will trade is how far memory costs pull the margin down from here, and what John Ternus's first outlook says about a December quarter launched without a standard iPhone.\nKey takeaways\nWhen. After the close on Monday 2 November 2026 ; call at 5 p.m. ET (22:00 UTC) . The quarter ended 26 September .\nThe guide. Revenue +9% to +11% on $102.5bn ; gross margin 47%–48% including about 1 point of tariff refunds. Consensus near $113.3bn and $1.99 EPS.\nThe cost. Memory prices rose in March, June and again in September. Ex-refunds, the margin guide is about 46%–47% , against 48.1% in June.\nThe cushion. Inventory went from $5.72bn to $11.09bn in nine months. Apple says that carry-in benefit fades after September.\nThe real number. The December-quarter guide — the first from the new CEO, for a holiday quarter that carried $85.3bn of iPhone a year ago.\nFor the rate backdrop that sets how mega-cap growth is valued, see how the eight majors are scoring on the live meter .\nWhen the report lands, and why the timing is unusual\nApple confirmed on its investor relations site that it will discuss fiscal fourth-quarter results on Monday 2 November at 2 p.m. Pacific, 5 p.m. Eastern. US daylight saving ends on Sunday 1 November, so the call starts at 22:00 UTC rather than the 21:00 UTC of Apple's July call — a small detail that matters to anyone watching futures from Europe or Asia.\nThe date itself is later than usual. Apple reported the equivalent quarter last year on 30 October, but this year it sits after the cluster of mega-cap reports — Microsoft and Alphabet on 28 October, Amazon on 29 October — and after the Federal Reserve's 28 October decision. By the time Apple publishes, the market will already have the cloud providers' spending plans and the Fed's signal on the rate path. That gives the report an unusual job: it is the cleanest read on what the AI build-out is doing to a company that buys the components rather than sells them.\nIt is also the first earnings call led by John Ternus. Apple announced on 20 April that Ternus, then head of hardware engineering, would succeed Tim Cook as chief executive on 1 September, with Cook becoming executive chairman. A leadership change reaches prices through guidance and capital allocation, not through who speaks first, so the call is the first occasion on which the new management's financial framing can be judged.\nWhat the guidance and the consensus imply\nFiscal Q4 (Jul–Sep)\nApple guidance / consensus\nFiscal Q4 2025 actual\nRevenue\n+9% to +11% (≈ $111.7bn–$113.7bn)\n$102.5bn\nConsensus revenue\nabout $113.3bn\n—\nGross margin\n47%–48% (incl. ≈1pt tariff refunds)\n47.2%\niPhone\nmid-teens growth\n$49.0bn\nServices\ngrowth similar to June quarter, FX-adjusted\n$28.75bn\nDiluted EPS\nconsensus about $1.99\n$1.85\nFX effect\n≈2.5pt sequential headwind to growth\n—\nYear-ago figures from Apple's fiscal Q4 2025 results ; guidance from the 30 July call accompanying its fiscal Q3 2026 results . Consensus from published analyst tallies, which differ slightly by compiler.\nTwo points sit inside that table. First, consensus revenue of about $113.3bn implies growth of roughly 10.6%, close to the top of the range. Apple has beaten consensus EPS in each of its last eight reports on published tallies, and the market has priced that habit in — a result at the midpoint would meet Apple's forecast and still read as a miss.\nSecond, the EPS consensus of $1.99 implies growth of about 7.6% on revenue growth of about 10.6%. That gap is the margin story in one line. Earnings are expected to grow more slowly than sales because each dollar of revenue is costing more to produce.\nRead the margin ex-refunds Apple's reported margins this year include one-off tariff refunds: about 2 points in the June quarter and about 1 point in the September guide. Strip them out and the June quarter was 48.1%, the midpoint of what Apple had guided, and the September guide becomes roughly 46%–47%. A headline margin of 47.5% would therefore be a lower underlying margin than a year ago, not a stable one.\nThe memory squeeze, and why it reaches Apple\nThe mechanism runs through a market most consumers never see. DRAM and NAND flash are made by a small number of producers, and the same factories now supply AI data centres, where high-bandwidth memory and server-grade DRAM earn far more per wafer. When hyperscalers bid for capacity, producers shift output towards them, and the price of the commodity memory that goes into phones and laptops rises with it.\nApple sits on the receiving end. On the July call, Cook compared the memory price environment to a once-in-a-century flood, said costs had risen in the March and June quarters and would rise again in September, and said Apple had reluctantly raised some iPad and Mac prices as a result. CFO Kevan Parekh went further: the memory cost increase was larger than the entire fall in gross margin from the June quarter to the September guide, meaning other factors partly offset it.\nAI capex Hyperscalers bid for DRAM and NAND capacity\n→\nMemory prices Producers favour data-centre parts; device memory gets dearer\n→\nApple's cost of sales Each iPhone, Mac and iPad costs more to build\n→\nMargin and EPS Earnings grow slower than revenue\nThis is the same capex wave that has driven chip stocks and dominated the Microsoft, Meta and Amazon reports, seen from the other side of the trade. For the companies spending the money, the question is return on capital. For Apple, it is input cost — which is why the 22 October sell-off in chip names and an Apple margin squeeze are two faces of one story, not separate ones. Our analysis of that session covers the spending side.\nThe inventory cushion — and why it is temporary\nApple's own balance sheet shows how it has been managing the squeeze. Inventories stood at $11.09bn on 27 June 2026, against $5.72bn at the end of the previous fiscal year — an increase of about 94% in nine months, for a company that has historically run very lean stocks. The filing does not break that figure down, but management said on the call that carry-in inventory would partly offset memory costs in the September quarter, which is consistent with buying components before prices rose further.\nThe same commentary carried the caveat that matters for the outlook: Apple expects the inventory benefit to decline after September as memory prices continue to rise. In plain terms, components bought earlier at lower prices are being used up, and their replacements cost more. That makes the September margin a partly cushioned number and the December guide the first that has to absorb more of the current price.\nThere is a second constraint on the supply side. Parekh said supply limits would increase significantly in the September quarter, and Cook tied them to the availability of the advanced chipmaking processes used for Apple's own processors rather than to any supplier failure. Demand for iPhone and Mac had run ahead of what those processes could produce. A supply-limited quarter is a different signal from a demand-limited one: it caps revenue in the period but says little about appetite.\nWhy the December guide matters more than the September result\nThe quarter being reported contains only a sliver of the new product cycle. The iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max went on sale on 18 September, eight days before the quarter closed on 26 September, and the f\n\n[Content truncated]",
